.TH OB-POST-UPGRADE 8 "September 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-post-upgrade \- finish an Open Bastion package upgrade on this host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-post-upgrade
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Installing the package is not always enough. Some of what a host needs lives
outside it:
.I /usr/local/sbin/ob-ssh-principals
is generated by
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8)
or
.BR ob-backend-setup (8),
so an upgrade replaces the daemon it talks to and leaves the helper as it was.
.PP
Until this command, the only cure was
.RB \(dq "re-run the setup script with the arguments you used before" \(dq
\(em and an operator who deployed through
.BR ob-builder (8)
months ago does not have them.
.PP
.B ob-post-upgrade
needs none. Everything it re\-asserts is derivable from the host: the shipped
helper text, the tmpfiles rule, the socket unit, and the ownership of the
fingerprint spool. It reads
.I /etc/open-bastion/openbastion.conf
for exactly one value \(em
.B node_role
\(em to choose the bastion or the backend helper, and writes nothing to it.
.PP
It is idempotent. Run it as often as you like; on a host that is already
current it reports that and changes nothing.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
.TP
.B It does not enrol, or re-enrol.
Re\-enrolling mints a
.B new
device id, which no backend allowlist contains, so every hop from this host
would then be refused. It is the most damaging thing an operator can do while
trying to repair one.
.TP
.B It does not touch your configuration.
Not
.IR openbastion.conf ,
not the server token, not
.IR sshd_config ,
not any PAM stack. Those carry decisions \(em a server group, a mode, an
.BI \-\-enable\- *
flag \(em and a command that cannot know them must not guess. To change a
decision, run the setup script.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Report what would change and change nothing. Does not require root.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Usage.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
the host is up to date, whether or not anything had to be done
.TP
.B 1
something could not be repaired, or this host has never been set up
.SH VERIFYING
Log in with a public key from elsewhere \(em a password login writes no drop
\(em then, as root:
.IP
.EX
find /run/open-bastion/ssh-fp \-name '*.fp' \-newermt '\-2 min'
.EE
.PP
It has to be a
.I fresh
drop: old ones are never cleaned up, so a listing that is merely non\-empty
proves nothing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8),
.BR ob-backend-setup (8),
.BR ob-fp-daemon (8),
.BR ob-fp-submit (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Linagora
